    "q-80605": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80605",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$",
      "worked_solution": "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
